For the decay

\[
\pi^+ \rightarrow \mu^+ + \nu
\]

with the pion initially at rest, take the neutrino mass as zero.

Given:

\[
m_\pi = 139.6\ \text{MeV}, \qquad m_\mu = 105.7\ \text{MeV}
\]

For a two-body decay at rest, the kinetic energies are:

\[
K_\mu = \frac{(m_\pi - m_\mu)^2}{2m_\pi}
\]

\[
K_\nu = \frac{m_\pi^2 - m_\mu^2}{2m_\pi}
\]

Calculate:

\[
m_\pi - m_\mu = 139.6 - 105.7 = 33.9\ \text{MeV}
\]

\[
K_\mu = \frac{(33.9)^2}{2(139.6)}
= \frac{1149.21}{279.2}
\approx 4.12\ \text{MeV}
\]

\[
K_\nu = \frac{(139.6)^2 - (105.7)^2}{2(139.6)}
\approx 29.8\ \text{MeV}
\]

So the kinetic energies are approximately:

\[
K_\mu \approx 4.12\ \text{MeV}, \qquad K_\nu \approx 29.8\ \text{MeV}
\]

